{
  "id": "COMPOUND:31141 - PROTEIN:6323",
  "verbalization": "Oleamide is implicated in the regulation of DNA polymerase iota through a multi-step mechanism involving the mitochondrial thioredoxin pathway. The evidence suggests that Oleamide acts as a target of multiple compounds, including [1-(4,8-Dimethoxy-1-methyl-2-oxoquinolin-3-yl)-3-methylbut-3-en-2-yl] hexadecanoate and CID 20842, which in turn target DNA polymerase iota. This indicates a potential pathway where Oleamide may influence the activity of thioredoxin, which is a key regulator of mitochondrial processes. The presence of multiple compounds with similar targets further supports the idea that Oleamide could modulate the activity of thioredoxin, potentially affecting DNA polymerase iota. The integration of these findings suggests that Oleamide may exert a biological effect on DNA polymerase iota through a mechanism involving the mitochondrial thioredoxin system, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
